        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 50 pint dehumidifier is the biggest portable you can buy, the right size for most damp basements and the size most people should start with. It is also the most confusingly labelled appliance in the hardware aisle: the pint rating changed in 2019, the old 70 pint machines are today's 50s, and the 4,500 square foot coverage claims are marketing rather than measurement. This guide picks six genuine current-test 50 pint units and explains what the numbers on the box actually mean.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="rating-change" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What "50 Pint" Means After the 2019 Rating Change
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number on the box is the amount of water a dehumidifier is rated to pull from the air in 24 hours, and since 2019 that number comes from a much colder test. The Department of Energy moved the rating conditions from 80°F and 60% relative humidity to 65°F and 60%, which is closer to a real basement. Cold air holds less water and the coil works harder, so the same machine produces a smaller number. A unit sold as 70 pints before the change is, almost exactly, today's 50 pint unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is not a trivia point. It means 50 pints is now the top of the portable range, where 70 was before, and it means the market is full of listings that mix the two scales. Honeywell's own spec sheet for the TP70AWKN puts both numbers in one title: a 50 pint dehumidifier, 70 pint under the 2012 DOE standard. The model number keeps the old figure; the capacity in the headline is the new one. Read further down a listing and you will often find the two numbers presented the other way round.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trap runs in the other direction too. Honeywell's smaller TP50AWKN was sold for years as a 50 pint dehumidifier, and is still listed that way, but under the 2019 test it is roughly a 30 to 32 pint machine with a 7 pint bucket and a 3,000 square foot claim. If you are buying a 50 pint dehumidifier for a basement and the model number has a 50 in it, check which test the 50 refers to. The fastest check is the ENERGY STAR product finder, which lists the measured capacity under the current procedure for every certified model.
          </p>

          <p>
