        <div id="square-footage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 4,500 Square Foot Claim vs Your Basement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every 50 pint dehumidifier on Amazon is marketed for 4,000 to 5,000 square feet. GE's listing says up to 5,000, Midea, TCL and Hisense say 4,500, and Honeywell says 4,000. None of these figures come from the DOE test, which measures pints per day and says nothing about floor area. They are marketing estimates for a moderately damp space, and in a genuinely wet basement they are two to three times too generous.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is that floor area is the wrong unit. A dehumidifier has to remove the water that keeps arriving, through the foundation walls, the slab, a dryer that vents badly, a sump pit without a lid and the outdoor air leaking in. A tight 1,000 square foot basement with a vapor barrier can need less capacity than a 600 square foot fieldstone cellar with a dirt floor in one corner. Square footage tells you how much air there is, not how much water is coming in.
          </p>

          <p>
            A more honest way to use the claims is to treat a 50 pint unit as right for roughly 1,500 to 2,500 square feet of basement that smells musty or shows condensation on cold-water pipes, and up to about 1,000 to 1,500 square feet of basement with visible wet patches, efflorescence or a history of seepage. Above that, two 35 or 50 pint units at either end of the space usually beat one machine pushing air across the whole floor, because portables do not circulate air very far.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unsure, run the cheapest test available. Put a $15 digital hygrometer in the basement for a week in summer. If the reading sits at 60% to 70%, a 50 pint unit will pull it to the 45% to 50% target comfortably. If it sits above 75% or the walls are visibly damp, fix the water source first, and see our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement sizing guide</a> for the worked examples, including the dampness correction.
          </p>

