        <div id="pump-or-gravity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Pump or Gravity Drain: The Decision That Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 50 pint dehumidifier in a damp basement in August can collect most of its rated output every day. The buckets on these units hold between 1.9 and 2.7 gallons, which is 15 to 22 pints, so on a muggy day a bucket can fill two or three times. Nobody empties a dehumidifier three times a day for long. The units that end up switched off in a corner are almost always the ones that were relying on a bucket.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the first question is not the brand, it is where the water goes. If there is a floor drain, a sump pit or a laundry standpipe lower than the dehumidifier's drain port, a gravity hose solves it for the price of a garden hose, and any bucket model on this list works. Set the unit on a sturdy stand or a couple of concrete blocks if you need the hose to run downhill, and keep the run short and without kinks.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there is no drain below the unit, buy a pump model. The Frigidaire FHDP5033Y1 and the Honeywell TPFIT50PWK both push water up through a hose to a sink or out a window, and both keep a gravity port as a backup. The pump often costs surprisingly little extra (one major retailer listed Frigidaire's pump model at the same $298 as its Wi-Fi bucket model) and adds a part that can fail, but it is the difference between a machine that runs all summer and a machine that is full by lunchtime. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> covers the add-on condensate pump route, which is cheaper if you already own a bucket model.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical note on pump models: neither Frigidaire nor Honeywell publishes a pump lift height on the retail listings we found, while Midea rates the Cube's pump at about 16 vertical feet. Plan for a modest rise, keep the tube free of loops that can trap air, and test the route before you leave the unit unattended. If the pump fails, both units fall back to a bucket and shut off when it is full, so you lose drying, not floor space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bucket-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bucket Size and How Often You Will Empty It
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are going to empty a bucket by hand, bucket size is the spec that decides how much you will hate the machine. The range on 50 pint units is wider than most buyers assume. The Frigidaire FHDP5033Y1 pump model has a 1.9 gallon bucket, the Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 lists 2.7 gallons, and the Midea Cube's expanding tank is listed by retailers at about 4.2 gallons, roughly double the Frigidaire.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the arithmetic for a damp week in July. A unit removing 30 pints a day, which is well below its rating and typical for a moderately damp basement, fills a 15 pint bucket twice a day, a 22 pint bucket about once and a half, and a 34 pint tank roughly once a day. That is the difference between a chore you do before work and after dinner and a chore you do once. If no one is home all day, a full bucket also means the unit has stopped drying hours before anyone notices.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the other half. Water weighs a little over 8 pounds a gallon, so a full 2.7 gallon bucket is about 22 pounds and a full Midea Cube tank is about 35 pounds, carried up a basement staircase. The Cube's tank sits low in the cabinet and has a handle, but it is not a light lift. If anyone in the house struggles with stairs, that alone is a reason to buy a pump model or set up a gravity hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every unit here shuts off automatically when the bucket is full, and the Wi-Fi models will tell your phone when that happens. The alert is genuinely useful, but it does not empty the bucket for you. If you are buying the Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 for the app and do not have a drain, understand that the app will mostly be telling you to go downstairs. Our <a href="/best-smart-dehumidifier/">smart dehumidifier guide</a> goes through which app features are worth having and which are not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="low-temp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cold Basements and Low-Temperature Operation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compressor dehumidifiers work by chilling a coil below the dew point of the air so water condenses on it. In a cool room the coil drops below freezing and frosts up, and a frosted coil stops collecting water. Every unit here has an automatic defrost cycle that pauses the compressor and runs the fan to clear the ice, but the colder the room, the more of its time the unit spends defrosting and the less it spends drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the minimum operating temperature on the spec sheet matters. Frigidaire rates the FHDD5034Y1 and the FHDP5033Y1 down to 41°F. GE's manual for the ADHE50BWF warns that below about 41°F it collects little or no water. TCL rates its H50D46W smart pump model to 41°F with freeze prevention. Hisense rates the AHD5024K1W lower, at 38°F, the coldest published figure in this group. Those are survival limits, not performance points: expect output to fall well before you reach them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The current 2019 test is run at 65°F, so the 50 pint rating is already a cool-basement figure. A basement that sits at 60°F to 65°F in summer will see roughly the rated output if the air is humid. One that sits at 50°F to 55°F will see noticeably less, and that is the range where Hisense's lower rating and a model that defrosts quickly earn their keep. Below about 50°F, a compressor unit is the wrong tool and a desiccant dehumidifier, which does not rely on a cold coil, starts to make sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most basements are humid in summer and dry in winter, when outdoor air holds little water and the furnace warms the space, so the cold-weather limit matters less than it looks for many homes. It matters a lot for unheated cellars, crawl spaces and garages. If that is your situation, read our guides to the <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">best crawl space dehumidifiers</a> and to <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">whether you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> before buying a portable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a 50 Pint Unit Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Frigidaire lists its 50 pint units at 470 watts. Run one for 12 hours a day and that is about 5.6 kWh, which at a typical US residential rate of around 17 cents per kWh comes to roughly 96 cents a day, or about $29 a month in a humid summer. In a basement where the humidistat cycles the compressor off for half the day, the figure is lower. Those are our arithmetic from the spec sheet watts, not a measured bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            ENERGY STAR gives a second, more standardized number. The certification records for the TCL and Honeywell TP-Fit 50 pint models list 516 kWh of annual energy use under the program's assumptions, which is around $88 a year at 17 cents. Efficiency is expressed as integrated energy factor in liters of water per kWh; the Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 and TCL H50D44W both list 2.01 L/kWh. Higher is better, and the difference between a certified and an older uncertified unit can be several dollars a month over a long season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two settings matter more than the brand. The first is the humidity target. Holding a basement at 45% instead of 50% makes the unit run noticeably longer for very little benefit; 50% is the usual target for a basement, and the guidance on <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> explains why going lower rarely helps. The second is the fan. Auto or Smart Dry modes that slow the fan as the room approaches the target trim both energy and noise.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a fuller picture, including how a 50 pint compares with 22 and 35 pint units on cost per pint removed, see our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a>. The short version is that an ENERGY STAR 50 pint machine removing a lot of water is cheaper per pint than a small unit running flat out, so undersizing to save power usually backfires.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-big" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When 50 Pints Is Too Much, and When It Is Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 50 pint unit is the right size for most basements, and buyers rarely regret going up from 35. But it is not always the right call. In a single bedroom, a bathroom or a small laundry room under about 500 square feet, a 50 pint machine reaches the target quickly, cycles off, and spends most of its life idle and loud. A 22 pint unit costs less, weighs about half as much and runs at a lower noise level, and for a room that only gets muggy, it is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 35 pint class sits in between, and for a finished, mostly dry lower level that only smells stale in summer, it is often the better buy: cheaper, a few pounds lighter and a little quieter. The price gap between a 35 and a 50 pint unit from the same brand is often $40 to $70, though, so if the space is borderline or you suspect the basement is wetter than it looks, the 50 pint unit is the safer bet. A bigger unit on a humidistat simply runs less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fifty pints is not enough when the basement is actually wet. If you can see water on the floor after rain, the walls are dark with damp or the sump runs often, no portable dehumidifier fixes that. The water needs to be stopped first with grading, gutters, sealing or drainage, and a dehumidifier then handles what is left. Two 50 pint units can cover a large basement in the meantime, but they are running cost, not a cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the very top end, a ducted whole-house unit rated around 70 to 100 pints under the current test, installed by an HVAC contractor, is the upgrade. It costs much more upfront but drains to a condensate line, moves far more air and runs quietly from a mechanical room. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> guide covers the larger options, and the <a href="/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/">whole-house guide</a> explains when ducted is worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Frigidaire, Midea, GE, Honeywell, Hisense and LG Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 50 pint market looks crowded, but most of it is a handful of large appliance makers selling similar compressor units with different cabinets, buckets and apps. Frigidaire's lineup is the most complete: a plain unit, a Wi-Fi unit and a Wi-Fi pump unit, with replacement parts and filters easy to find. Its 1-year warranty is the weak point. The Frigidaire 50 pint with Wi-Fi is our top pick because it combines the biggest conventional bucket, three speeds and a low-temperature rating with an app that costs nothing extra.
          </p>

          <p>
            Midea's Cube is the only genuinely different design, trading a normal bucket for an expanding tank, and it is the one to buy if you will be emptying by hand. GE's ADHE50 series is solid and plain, with Smart Dry fan control and a Wi-Fi option, but GE is more conservative about cold operation than Hisense. Hisense undercuts the big names on price, offers the longest warranty here at 2 years, and publishes the coldest operating figure. Honeywell's line is the most confusing to shop because of its mix of old and new pint ratings; the TPFIT50PWK is a genuine current-test 50 pint unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            LG's PuriCare 50 pint units are worth knowing about. The older UD501KOG5 and UD501KOJ5 are hard to find new, and LG's current 50 pint model is the PuriCare DT501BKR0, a Wi-Fi unit controlled through LG ThinQ. We cover it in the <a href="/best-smart-dehumidifier/">smart dehumidifier guide</a> rather than here, because the app is most of what you pay extra for. We also left out the dozens of no-name 70, 80 and 120 pint listings from brands without a published DOE rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are loyal to one brand, the batch of brand guides goes deeper: the <a href="/midea-cube-dehumidifier-review/">Midea Cube review</a> compares the 20, 35 and 50 pint Cubes, and the <a href="/ge-dehumidifier-review/">GE dehumidifier review</a> covers the full GE range, including the pump and Wi-Fi models. For everyone else, the deciding factors are still drainage, bucket size and cold-room rating, in that order.
          </p>
        </div>

