        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a 70 pint listing with a 50 pint one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Since 2019 the DOE test runs at 65°F instead of 80°F, so a machine that was rated at 70 pints is now rated at about 50. A cheap 70 pint listing is usually the same size machine on the old scale, not a bigger one. Check the ENERGY STAR product finder for the current figure before you compare prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Honeywell "50 pint" that is really a 30</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some Honeywell units still sold as 50 pint dehumidifiers, such as the TP50AWKN, are rated at roughly 30 to 32 pints under the 2019 test. If the model number has a 50 in it, read the listing for the DOE standard. Honeywell's older genuine 50 pint units carry a 70 in the model number (TP70AWKN, TP70PWKN); the newer TP-Fit TPFIT50PWK is a true current-test 50.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the square footage on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 4,000 to 5,000 square foot claims are not part of the DOE test. A damp basement of 1,500 to 2,500 square feet is a realistic job for one 50 pint unit; a wet, seeping one of more than about 1,500 square feet needs two units or a water fix first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on a bucket in a damp basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50 pint unit can fill a 15 to 22 pint bucket two or three times on a humid summer day, and once it is full it stops drying. If there is no floor drain below the unit, buy a pump model or an add-on condensate pump rather than planning to empty it by hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the humidity target too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Holding a basement at 40% or 45% makes the compressor run far longer for little extra benefit and can push a cold-room unit into constant defrost. Fifty percent is the usual basement target. Use the humidistat and an auto fan mode rather than running the unit continuously.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
