          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50 pint dehumidifier the same as a 70 pint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. In 2019 the Department of Energy changed the test from 80°F to 65°F, which lowers the rating. A dehumidifier sold as 70 pints under the old test is rated at roughly 50 pints under the new one. Some brands, such as Honeywell, kept the old figure in the model number, so read the listing for which standard the pint number uses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a 50 pint dehumidifier cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers claim 4,000 to 5,000 square feet, but that is a marketing estimate for a moderately damp space. For a basement that smells musty or shows condensation, plan on about 1,500 to 2,500 square feet per 50 pint unit, and 1,000 to 1,500 for a basement with visible damp or seepage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50 pint dehumidifier big enough for a basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basements, yes. It is the largest portable size under the current DOE test and handles a typical damp basement of 1,500 to 2,500 square feet. A very large or genuinely wet basement may need two units placed at either end, or a ducted whole-house dehumidifier, once the source of water has been dealt with.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 50 pint dehumidifier with a pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if there is no floor drain, sump or standpipe below the unit's drain port. A gravity hose is cheaper and has nothing to fail. If you would have to lift the water to a sink or out a window, a built-in pump such as the Frigidaire FHDP5033Y1 or Honeywell TPFIT50PWK is the reliable answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often will I have to empty a 50 pint dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the bucket and how damp the space is. Buckets on 50 pint units hold 15 to 22 pints, and the Midea Cube's tank about 34. A unit removing 30 pints a day fills a 15 pint bucket twice a day and the Cube about once. A hose or pump avoids emptying altogether.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 50 pint dehumidifier work in a cold basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Down to a point. Most are rated to about 41°F and the Hisense AHD5024K1W to 38°F, but output falls well before those limits as the coil spends more time defrosting. Between 50°F and 60°F expect less than the rating. In an unheated space colder than about 50°F, a desiccant dehumidifier is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a 50 pint dehumidifier cost to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 470 watt unit running 12 hours a day uses about 5.6 kWh, roughly 96 cents a day at 17 cents per kWh, which is about $29 for a humid month. ENERGY STAR records for some 50 pint models list 516 kWh a year, around $88. Setting the humidistat to 50% rather than lower keeps the bill down.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

